"""Chapter 18 deck notes, version 3.0.0: the speaker notes of the 53 slides, written for the rulings of 2026-11-14. Each note is full sentences, 60 to 140 words
(at least 30 on the title, part and resources slides); a code slide's note says what to point at and what the printed result shows; every note leads into the next slide.
No label prefixes, no prompt markers, no lambda. The facts are those of the slide: the results quoted are what the example printed when the deck was built."""
__version__ = "3.0.0"

NOTES = {
"title": "Welcome to chapter 18, CSV, JSON and XML files. Point at the title and then at the subtitle: three ways to keep data as plain text, and the standard library calls that read and write each one. Every example on the following slides builds its own small data in memory or in a temporary folder, so nothing depends on files from the book. The same examples stand, in this order, in the Slide code tab of the chapter page, where you can edit and run them. Next, the route slide shows the six parts of the lecture and the slides each one covers.",
"route": "Point at the table: six parts, the slide range of each, how many code examples it holds, and what you will run in it. Read the first column as the story of the chapter: CSV reading, CSV writing, Project 13, JSON, XML, and then the practice program that turns Excel sheets into CSV files. The last row gathers the closing slides, which collect the calls, the changes since the book and the links. Use the ranges to jump around when a student asks about one format. Next come the two questions that we carry through every part and answer at the end.",
"questions": "Point at the two cards, one at a time. The first asks how to read and write a table, a record and a tree with the standard library, and what each format loses on the way. The second asks which printed outputs, programs and statements of the book differ in Python today. Under each question the slide says on which slide the answer comes back, so students know the lecture ends with a real answer. Keep the questions in mind while the code runs: what did this call keep, and what did it change? Next, part one starts with reading CSV files.",
"divider1": "Part one is reading CSV files. Point at the four small cards under the title: reader, list, line number and next. A CSV file is records on lines, and each record is a list of fields. The reader cuts the fields for you, a for loop takes one row at a time, the line number counts lines, and next takes a header row off. Students who tried to split on commas will see on the code slides why that is not enough. Next, an everyday picture of a CSV file before any code.",
"A1": "Point at the headline picture at the top: a CSV file is a paper register, one line for each entry, with commas as the column lines. Then read the small rows below it, each pairing a term of the chapter with an everyday picture, such as the csv module as the clerk who knows the register's rules. Reading cuts lines into columns, and writing copies entries into a new register. Ask students to name one thing in their own life that is a register in this sense. Next, a short story about where comma-separated values came from.",
"ibm": "Point at the photograph on the left: an IBM System/360 console and tape drives in a museum, the machine family of OS/360, not the compiler itself. The story is in three short paragraphs. In 1972 the Fortran compiler for these mainframes already read values separated by commas, and the name CSV was in use by 1983. The lesson comes in the last paragraph: quotes were the later answer, and csv.reader keeps a comma inside quotes in one field. The link at the bottom opens the Wikipedia page. Next, the first code slide shows exactly that problem.",
"csv_split": "Point at the printed lines under the code: split gives four pieces, because it cuts at the comma inside the quotes, and reader gives the three fields that were meant. The line holds a name, an age and a city with a comma in it, so the third field is wrapped in double quotes. That is why the csv module exists: a quoted field may contain a comma. Point at the last printed line as well: two records of two fields, a file as a list of records and a record as a list of fields. Next, we read a whole file with reader and list.",
"reader_list": "Point at the code on the left: a small stand-in file is written to a temporary folder, opened, passed to csv.reader, and turned into a list of rows. On the right the printed result shows the rows, each a list of strings, and then one value picked by row and column. Point at the last printed word: str. Even the age 30 comes back as text, so you convert numbers yourself when you need them. Next, a for loop reads the same kind of data one row at a time, and shows what the reader keeps.",
"for_loop": "Point at the printed rows: the loop shows each row with its line number, and the numbers jump from one to three because one record has a line break inside quotes. The line number counts lines of the file, not records. Then point at the last two printed lines: a second pass over the reader gives an empty list, and the reader has no length. A reader can be used once, so call list when you need to keep the rows. The loop itself holds only one row in memory. Next, next skips a header row and shows where the line number test fails.",
"next_header": "Point at the first two printed lines: next takes the header off the reader, and what remains is the data without it. Then point at the last line, where the header itself spans two lines because it holds a line break in quotes. The book's test, line number equal to one, fails here: the header record ends on line two, so the header is kept as data. Next is the safer habit. Taking the first row with next does not depend on how many lines it spans. Next, part two turns around and writes CSV files.",
"divider2": "Part two is writing CSV files. Point at the four cards: writer, delimiter, newline and encoding. csv.writer writes lists and DictWriter writes dictionaries, and a tab is just another delimiter. The newline argument stops Windows from doubling the line ends, and the files that Excel saves as UTF-8 start with a byte order mark that you have to know about. Each of these has a small example that you can run and change. Next, an everyday picture for tabs, headers and line ends.",
"A2": "Point at the headline picture: a header row is like the heading strip of a register. It names the columns and holds no entry. Then read down the rows below it. A tab instead of a comma changes only the separator. Windows without newline set to the empty string presses return twice, so a blank line follows every entry, and that is the picture to remember when the double-spaced file appears later. Ask students what the heading strip would be in a file they have opened in Excel. Next, the story of how CSV was finally written down.",
"rfc": "Point at the photograph: a meeting room of the Internet Engineering Task Force, the body behind the RFC series. It does not show the author. The story says that in October 2005, after more than thirty years of CSV files, Yakov Shafranovich wrote RFC 4180, which documents the common format and registers the type text slash csv. The memo is informational, not a standard, which is why programs still read CSV in many ways. Point at the last paragraph: records end in carriage return and line feed. Next, the writer shows that ending in bytes.",
"writer_basics": "Point at the last printed line, the bytes: every record ends with a carriage return and a line feed, the CRLF of the RFC. Above it the first lines show how many characters each writerow call wrote. Then point at the second record in the bytes: the field with a comma is wrapped in double quotes, while numbers and None have become plain text, with None as an empty field. A StringIO stands in for the open file with newline set to the empty string. Next, delimiter and line terminator change the shape of the file.",
"tabs": "Point at the bytes first: the fields are separated by a tab and the records end in a plain line feed. Two arguments did that. delimiter changes the character between fields, and lineterminator changes what ends a record. Words with spaces need no quotes when the delimiter is a tab. Then point at the list on the last line: reading the bytes back with the same delimiter returns the rows that were written. The rule is simple. Write and read with the same settings. Next comes the famous double-spaced file and the argument that prevents it.",
"newline_blank": "Point at the two printed lines. The first starts with an empty string and shows the bytes a, b, carriage return, line feed: two clean records. The second, from the Windows stand-in, shows carriage return, carriage return, line feed, so a reader finds an empty record after every row. That is the double spacing of the book's figure. A text wrapper asked for CRLF plays Windows here, so this is a stand-in and not a real Windows run. The fix is the argument newline set to the empty string when you open the file. Next, six quoting constants write one row.",
"quoting": "Point at the six printed lines: the same row, written once with each quoting constant. The default, minimal, quotes only the field with the comma. All quotes every field, non-numeric leaves numbers bare, and none escapes the comma with a backslash instead of quoting it. The two newer constants, strings and not-null, treat None and the empty string differently, so compare their last two fields. Ask which you would choose for a file that another program must read. Next, the encoding that the chapter never names, and a three-byte mark at the start of a file.",
"encoding_bom": "Point at the first printed line: the three bytes of the byte order mark come before the first letter of the file, because Excel's CSV UTF-8 option writes them. Decoded as plain utf-8 the mark stays as an invisible first character, and the last line shows it glued to the first key, so a lookup of Name would raise a KeyError. The codec utf-8-sig removes it. The habit to take away is to pass an encoding to open every time. Next, DictReader and DictWriter give every row its column names.",
"dict_rw": "Point at the first two printed dictionaries: DictReader used the first row as keys, and the short row for Bob got None for the missing value. Then point at the last line: DictWriter wrote the header from its field names and filled a missing key with an empty field, so Eve's row ends in a comma. Dictionaries make rows readable by name instead of by position, which survives a reordered column. Next, part three puts the pieces together in Project 13, which removes the header row from every CSV file in a folder.",
"divider3": "Part three is Project 13, removing the header row. Point at the four cards: three steps and a pointer to similar programs. Step one loops through the files of a folder, step two reads a file and skips its header row, and step three writes the other rows to a new file. The same plan serves totals, merges and filters. Each step has its own small example here, so you can see what every piece does before the whole program runs. Next, an everyday picture of the project.",
"A3": "Point at the headline picture: Project 13 is a clerk who tears the heading strip off every register in a drawer. The rows below tie the three steps to that picture: open the drawer and go through the registers, read one and set the strip aside, copy the rest onto a new register. Notice that the original registers stay untouched in the drawer; the program writes new files into another folder. Ask what could go wrong if the program wrote over the original. Next, step one: looping through the files.",
"p13_loop": "Point at the printed lines: only a.csv and b.csv are announced. Four empty files stand in for the project's folder, one of them a text file and one with a capital extension, and the loop skips both because the test on the ending is case sensitive. So C.CSV is skipped, which surprises people. Sorting the names first makes the order the same on every machine. If you want capitals to count, lower the name before testing it. Next, step two reads a file and skips its first row.",
"p13_read": "Point at the first printed line: the header is gone and one data row is left. The second line shows an empty file, where next returned None instead of raising an error. That is the reason for the second argument of next: without it, an empty file would stop the whole program with StopIteration. A file with only a header gives an empty list, which is a correct answer. Next, step three writes the remaining rows to a new file and counts the lines before and after.",
"p13_write": "Point at the two printed lines: each file shows its line count before and after, three to two and two to one. Every file is exactly one line shorter, which is the proof that only the header row was removed. The code skips the header with next, keeps the other rows in a list, and writes them with writerows to a file of the same name in a new folder. Check the counts rather than the look of the files. Next, a similar program shows how far the same plan reaches.",
"col_total": "Point at the dictionary that is printed: one running total for each region, North 180, South 80 and East 40. The values of a CSV file are text, so int converts them before adding, and the dictionary keeps one total per region. The plan is the same as Project 13: loop, read, then do something with each row. Only the last step differs, and that is why small CSV programs are quick to write. Next, part four moves from tables to records and starts with JSON.",
"divider4": "Part four is JSON. Point at the four cards: loads, dumps, value types and strict syntax. JSON has six kinds of value: strings, numbers, booleans, null, lists and dictionaries. loads reads JSON text into Python values, dumps writes Python values as JSON text, and a round trip changes some of them, for example tuples become lists. The syntax is strict, and a trailing comma is an error. The same small record is shown in all three formats first. Next, an everyday picture for JSON.",
"A4": "Point at the headline picture: JSON is a labelled form, where every blank has a name and the value is written next to it. Then read the rows below. One address can be a line in a register, a labelled form or a tree of tags, and the three pictures are the three formats of this chapter. loads reads a form, dumps fills one in, and the json module refuses a badly written form instead of guessing. Ask students which of the three they find easiest to read by eye. Next, the story of where the name Ajax came from.",
"ajax": "Point at the portrait on the left, and at the date in the title. On 18 February 2005 Jesse James Garrett named Ajax in an essay on the blog of his firm, Adaptive Path, with Google Suggest and Google Maps as his examples. The name is short for Asynchronous JavaScript and XML. Point at the last paragraph: XML is the X of Ajax, and this chapter reads the plain text, in JSON and XML, that a browser and a server pass to each other. The link opens the essay's background. Next, one record is written in all three formats.",
"formats_compare": "Point at the three written forms of the same two people, and then at the printed counts: 28, 58 and 111 characters. CSV is the smallest because it names the columns once. JSON repeats the keys for every person, and XML repeats opening and closing tags, but both carry their structure in every record. That is the price of structure. Pick the format by what the reader needs, not by habit. Next, loads reads JSON text and shows the six types of value.",
"json_loads": "Point at the printed dictionary: loads read the text and returned a dictionary with a list inside. Then point at the translations, null became None and true became True, and at the list of types on the last line, which shows one type for each value: str, int, NoneType, bool and list. The numbers kept their kind, so 30 is an integer here and not text, unlike the CSV values earlier. Next, dumps goes the other way, and the round trip will not return the original.",
"json_dumps": "Point at the first printed line, the JSON text: the tuple became a list, the number key one became the text one, and the letter in Istanbul was escaped. Then point at the second line, the value read back. It is not the original, because the tuple is now a list and the key is a string. The third line says False: the round trip changed the data. The last lines show indent at work, which spreads the text over several lines for people. Next, what json.loads refuses.",
"json_strict": "Point at the printed lines, one for each text that loads was given. A trailing comma, single quotes, Python's None and an empty text are all refused, each with a message and the column where the parser stopped. Then point at the line for NaN: it is accepted, although JSON has no NaN. That is a known gap between the standard and the module, so do not rely on it for data from other people. Next, dump and load work with files and bring the byte order mark back.",
"json_files": "Point at the bytes on the first line: the file as stored, with the letter in Istanbul as two bytes, because ensure_ascii was turned off. Then point at the True: load read the file back and this time the value equals the original, because it holds only types that survive. The last line shows the byte order mark. Bytes that begin with it are accepted, but a string that begins with it is refused. Next, part five leaves tables and records and turns to trees, with XML.",
"divider5": "Part five is XML. Point at the four cards: ElementTree, find, xmltodict and defusedxml. XML is a tree of elements, and ElementTree is the standard library's way to read and write that tree. Text is always a string or None. xmltodict turns a document into a dictionary, which is quick to read but easy to misread, and XML that comes from someone else needs defusedxml. The part has an everyday picture, two stories and five code slides. Next, the picture of boxes inside boxes.",
"A5": "Point at the headline picture: XML is a family tree written in labelled boxes, where every box has a name and may hold smaller boxes. ElementTree opens and packs the boxes, and xmltodict lays them out as one notebook page of names and values. Notice that the tags must match, like a lid for every box. That strictness is a theme of this part. Ask students to draw a person with a name and two phones as boxes inside boxes. Next, the story of how XML 1.0 was published.",
"xml": "Point at the portrait of Jon Bosak. XML 1.0 became a W3C Recommendation on 10 February 1998, after work that began in mid-1996, and Bosak recruited collaborators who knew both SGML and the web. The last paragraph explains the code that follows: the rules are strict on purpose, with one root and matching tags and case, and ElementTree raises ParseError when they break. A strict language is easy for a program to check. The link opens the Wikipedia page. Next, reading an XML document with ElementTree.",
"et_read": "Point at the printed lines in order. The root is a person with five children, the name, the age, the car and two phones. The name text is Alice Doe, and the empty car element has the text None. The two phone attributes are read with get, mobile and work. The last line shows str and 31: text is always a string, so convert numbers yourself. Looping over an element gives its children, and findall collects every match. Next, whitespace and broken XML show how strict the parser is.",
"et_traps": "Point at the first printed line: the line break and spaces between the tags are kept, as the text of the parent and the tail of the child, so whitespace is data. Then point at the three error lines. A closing tag that does not match, a second root and a capital letter in the wrong place all raise ParseError, each with a line and a column. XML is strict, and a program can rely on that. Next, writing a tree: Element, SubElement and tostring.",
"et_write": "Point at the first printed line: one long line of XML, in which the less-than sign and the ampersand of the note were escaped for us. Then point at the indented version below it, where indent added line breaks and spaces for people to read. Element makes the root and SubElement adds each child. Writing to a file is one more call, and xml_declaration adds the first line that starts an XML file. Next, namespaces, where names are not what they seem.",
"et_nil": "Point at the first printed dictionary: the attribute nil appears under its full name, with the web address in braces, and ElementTree did not interpret it. The next line shows the text is simply None. The prefix xsi is only a short name for an address declared in the document. Then point at the last line: an element in a namespace is written with the address in braces, so i and a colon i are different elements. Next, xmltodict reads the same kind of document as a dictionary.",
"xmltodict_rt": "Point at the first printed dictionary: the attribute of phone has an at sign in front of its key, and its text sits under the key hash text. Then compare the next two lines: one b child gives a string, and two give a list, so a program must expect both. The last lines show unparse writing the dictionary back as XML, and the round trip returns the same dictionary. Everything in it is a string. Next, a library with a person behind it.",
"pylib": "Point at the picture: it is the history comment at the top of Fredrik Lundh's ElementTree file, drawn as text, because no free portrait of him was found. Lundh created ElementTree on 20 October 2001, and it was added to the Python project on 12 December 2005. He kept the copyright and later handed its upkeep to the Python developers. Point at the last paragraph: you write import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET, and a module has a person behind it. Next, what happens when XML comes from someone else.",
"xml_security": "Point at the two printed lines. The standard parser expands the entities inside the document and returns 30 characters from a few short lines, while defusedxml refuses the entities at once. Entity b stands for ten copies of entity a, so each further level multiplies the size by ten. This is a tiny cousin of the billion laughs attack. Never parse XML from someone else without checking the parser. Next, the big story of that attack.",
"laughs": "Point at the picture of server racks in a data centre. It shows the kind of machine such a file can exhaust and does not show an attack. The story says a document under 1 KB can have entities that expand to a billion copies of lol, almost 3 gigabytes. It was first reported as early as 2002 and widely addressed from 2008. The last paragraph ties it to the code: defusedxml refuses entities, as the last example showed. Next, part six, the practice program that turns Excel into CSV.",
"divider6": "Part six is the practice program, Excel to CSV. Point at the four cards: openpyxl, sheets, iter rows and the final check. The book's skeleton is a to-do list, and the two examples finish it. A workbook holds sheets, a sheet holds rows, and the program writes one CSV file for each sheet. Then it lists the files and reads them back to check. Next, an everyday picture of the converter.",
"A6": "Point at the headline picture: the converter is a copy clerk who writes each sheet of a ledger on a card of its own. openpyxl opens the workbook without Excel. Read the other rows, including shelve, which is a sealed shelf of Python objects and not a text format. Ask students why one CSV file per sheet is needed: a CSV file has no place for a second sheet. Next, the first example builds the workbook that the converter will read.",
"xl_make": "Point at the printed line: the workbook has two sheets, Sales and Costs, and a cell read back gives the number 120. The code made a new workbook with one sheet, appended rows after the last one, added a second sheet with create_sheet, saved into memory and loaded it again. The numbers stayed numbers, which a CSV file cannot promise. This workbook stands in for the book's spreadsheet files. Next, the converter loops over the sheets and writes the CSV files.",
"xl_convert": "Point at the two printed lines: book-Sales.csv with two rows and book-Costs.csv with one. The loop goes over the sheets, writes a file for each, named after the workbook and the sheet, and iter rows with values only gives plain values to write. The check at the end lists the files and reads them back, so the program proves its own result. Next, a table of the calls of the whole chapter.",
"tools": "Point at the first column of the table: every call of the chapter, then what it does and what to know about it, then the slide that shows it. Run your finger down the third column, because it holds the traps, such as opening reader files with newline set to the empty string, and every value being text. Reads change nothing, and writes make new text or a new file. This table answers the first question of the lecture. Next, what differs from the book today.",
"current": "Point at the left column: what the book says. The middle column says what is true today, and the last gives the slide that shows it. Run the book's lines first and then compare them with these rows. This slide answers the second question of the lecture. For example, the book advises opening reader files in binary mode, while today you open them in text mode with newline set to the empty string, and a binary reader raises an error. The research behind the table is dated and names its versions. Next, the summary.",
"summary": "Point at the five sentences on the left and at the chart on the right, which counts the code examples in each part. CSV is lines of fields, and the reader splits them with the newline argument right. JSON has six value types and a strict syntax, and a round trip changes tuples. XML is a tree and all its text is strings. XML from someone else needs defusedxml, and one CSV file is written for each sheet. A summary is a packing list. Next, practice questions.",
"practice": "Point at the five questions, one after the other. Ask students to predict the answer on paper first, then run the code on the chapter page and compare. The questions are small on purpose, and each answer fits in a few lines you can run. The practice programs are the recital pieces after the scales. Pick one question that scared you most and do it first. Next, the last slide gathers the resources.",
"resources": "Point at the link cards: the chapter of the book, the documentation of the csv, json and xml modules, the RFC, and the pages behind the stories of this lecture. Every link was checked when the chapter was prepared. Read the card titles aloud and tell students which one you would read first. Pick one link and read it before the next class. This is the last slide of the chapter, and the same examples wait for you in the Slide code tab of the page.",
}

STORY_NOTES = {k: NOTES[k] for k in ("ibm", "rfc", "ajax", "xml", "pylib", "laughs")}
if __name__ == "__main__":
    bad = [(k, len(v.split())) for k, v in NOTES.items() if not (60 <= len(v.split()) <= 140)]
    print(len(NOTES), "notes; outside 60-140:", bad)
